DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       zzssttdd is a fast lossless  compression  algorithm  and  data  compression
       tool,  with  command  line syntax similar to ggzziipp ((11)) and xxzz ((11)). It is
       based on the LLZZ7777 family, with further FSE & huff0 entropy stages. zzssttdd
       offers  highly configurable compression speed, with fast modes at > 200
       MB/s per core, and strong modes nearing  lzma  compression  ratios.  It
       also features a very fast decoder, with speeds > 500 MB/s per core.

       zzssttdd command line syntax is generally similar to gzip, but features the
       following differences :

       •   Source files are preserved by default. It´s possible to remove them
           automatically by using the ----rrmm command.

       •   When  compressing  a  single file, zzssttdd displays progress notifica‐
           tions and result summary by default. Use --qq to turn them off.

       •   zzssttdd does not accept input from console, but  it  properly  accepts
           ssttddiinn when it´s not the console.

       •   zzssttdd  displays a short help page when command line is an error. Use
           --qq to turn it off.



       zzssttdd compresses or decompresses each _f_i_l_e according to the selected op‐
       eration mode. If no _f_i_l_e_s are given or _f_i_l_e is --, zzssttdd reads from stan‐
       dard input and writes the processed data to standard output. zzssttdd  will
       refuse  to write compressed data to standard output if it is a terminal
       : it will display an error message and skip the _f_i_l_e.  Similarly,  zzssttdd
       will refuse to read compressed data from standard input if it is a ter‐
       minal.

       Unless ----ssttddoouutt or --oo is specified, _f_i_l_e_s are written  to  a  new  file
       whose name is derived from the source _f_i_l_e name:

       •   When  compressing,  the suffix ..zzsstt is appended to the source file‐
           name to get the target filename.

       •   When decompressing, the ..zzsstt suffix  is  removed  from  the  source
           filename to get the target filename



