       tions.   Therefore,  this  avenue  is  intentionally  restricted.  Only
       ZZSSTTDD__CCLLEEVVEELL and ZZSSTTDD__NNBBTTHHRREEAADDSS are currently supported.  They  set  the
       compression  level and number of threads to use during compression, re‐
       spectively.

       ZZSSTTDD__CCLLEEVVEELL can be used to set the level between 1 and 19 (the "normal"
       range).  If the value of ZZSSTTDD__CCLLEEVVEELL is not a valid integer, it will be
       ignored with a warning message. ZZSSTTDD__CCLLEEVVEELL just replaces  the  default
       compression level (33).

       ZZSSTTDD__NNBBTTHHRREEAADDSS  can  be used to set the number of threads zzssttdd will at‐
       tempt to use during compression. If the value of ZZSSTTDD__NNBBTTHHRREEAADDSS is  not
       a  valid  unsigned  integer, it will be ignored with a warning message.
       ZZSSTTDD__NNBBTTHHRREEAADDSS has a default value of (11), and is capped at  ZSTDMT_NB‐
       WORKERS_MAX==200.  zzssttdd  must  be compiled with multithread support for
       this to have any effect.

       They can both be overridden by corresponding command line arguments: --##
       for compression level and --TT## for number of compression threads.

